From: Gleb Pesin <dormancygrace@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] crypto: riscv - scalar GHASH for the T-Head C906

GHASH for lib/crypto on T-Head cores (detected by XTheadVector), such as the
C906 of the SG2002, in scalar code. The C906 multiplier takes about four
cycles per mul/mulhu, unpipelined, once an operand has bits above 31, and one
pipelined cycle when both fit in 32 bits; the generic code multiplies 64-bit
operands. Here the 128x128 carryless product is nine 32x32 ones (two levels
of Karatsuba), each sixteen multiplies of operands with their bits four apart,
all below 2^32, which also makes the multiply timing independent of the data.
ghash_preparekey() precomputes the key operands in struct ghash_key. The
assembly is generated and self-tested by
firmware/cpu/go-crypto-c906/gen_ghash_linux.py.

No vector state is used, so it also runs where may_use_simd() is false.
CRYPTO_LIB_GF128HASH_ARCH now also defaults to y with RISCV_ISA_XTHEADVECTOR;
the Zvkg backend keeps its previous conditions and takes precedence.

